; Syntax is a value, and `eval` needed no operator.
;
; `'A` answers with A as syntax. A name holds it, `$` interpolates it, and
; calling that name RUNS it — which is eval, spelled with two operators the
; language already had.
;
; Three calls: the two stages of the evaluated arrow, and the report that says
; what was held. The count is the proof that holding a program costs nothing and
; running it costs exactly what the program costs.
; expect: calls 3
; expect: clean
; expect: contains held: (-> "Answer with exactly the word: one"
; expect: answers
(= plan '(-> "Answer with exactly the word: one"
             "Reply with exactly the word: two")
   ((plan)
    ($ "Reply with exactly the word: done. This was held: " plan)))
